meta {
  name: What this caller may read
  type: http
  seq: 16
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/library/scopes
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.unrestricted: eq true
  res.body.count: eq 1
  res.body.labels[0]: contains unrestricted_
}

docs {
  The caller's readable set, written out as labels. This is the cleanest view
  of what class-wise scoping actually is, because it is the same set the
  retrieval engine is handed when a search runs: what a caller may find is
  decided before the search, not filtered out of its results.

  A label is a prefix and an id. school_ for everything at the school, grade_,
  section_ and offering_ for the three anchors a content item can carry, and
  person_ for items whose audience is their owner. unrestricted_ is the
  wildcard that matches all of them, and it is what this collection sees,
  because it runs as an unrestricted caller: one label, unrestricted true.

  A class teacher's set is the interesting one -- school_, grade_ for the
  grades they teach, one section_ per section, one offering_ per subject they
  teach in it, and their own person_. Seeing that here needs a real teacher
  token, which is the same gap the README notes about row-level narrowing.

  Two failures worth knowing, both 409 rather than an empty set, because an
  empty set reads as "you may see nothing" and would silently return no
  results:

  scope_unresolved              the set could not be worked out at all
  scope_pinned_unexpressible    the grant carries a constraint that cannot be
                                written as labels, so an operator has to widen
                                or remove it
}
